WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.120
Hi, everybody.

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:04.690
My name is Danielle Olson, and
I'm a fourth year PhD candidate

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:07.750
at the MIT Computer Science
and Artificial Intelligence

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:08.920
Laboratory.

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:11.860
I also work as a research
assistant for the MIT Center

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:14.770
for Advanced Virtuality,
directed by professor Fox

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:15.910
Harrell.

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:19.050
Today it's my pleasure to be
joined by Karim Ben Khalifa,

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:21.250
for MIT CAST 3 Questions series.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:26.490
All right, so I would
love for you to just give

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:29.580
an introduction to the
audience of who you are

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:32.520
and where your
work has taken you.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:34.262
So I am in Berlin.

00:00:34.262 --> 00:00:35.470
My name is Karim Ben Khalifa.

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:37.470
This is my base here.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:40.990
And I've been a war
correspondent for about 15,

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:44.970
18 years, depending on how
you see that at the end.

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:49.380
And I've done a project
called The Enemy, which

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:54.000
started developing when I
was at Open Doc Lab at MIT,

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.810
and then worked with
Professor Fox Harrell and CAST

00:00:57.810 --> 00:00:59.430
to develop that further.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:03.258
That became a full-blown
experience after.

00:01:03.258 --> 00:01:03.925
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:01:03.925 --> 00:01:07.404
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:15.356 --> 00:01:18.835
- You're too close to
really [INAUDIBLE]..

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:38.710
- The Enemy was born
out of my frustration.

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:41.680
As a photojournalist
and war correspondent

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:45.490
for almost 20 years, I
have photographed conflicts

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:49.045
and witnessed the consequences
of huge geopolitical shifts.

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:57.830
When I became a
father, I simply knew

00:01:57.830 --> 00:01:59.930
I could not keep working
on the front lines.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:08.114
Yet I was not done trying
to understand wars.

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:24.530
- [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:35.910
- My friends in Israel, when
they know I'm heading for Gaza,

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:40.070
couldn't help themselves but to
wish me luck and to stay safe.

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:43.550
They believe a lot of people
in Gaza are irrational.

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:46.370
Also, when I spend
weeks working in Gaza,

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:48.740
and I'm about to
return to Israel,

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:50.690
my Palestinian
friends are telling me

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:52.302
the exact same thing--

00:02:52.302 --> 00:02:53.010
be careful there.

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:03.290
The project is rooted
in my experience

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:06.770
as a war photographer, going
from one side of the front line

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:10.740
to the other and finding that
the fighters' dreams, hopes,

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:13.070
and nightmares are
often more similar

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:15.180
than they are different.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.630
So there is a bigger
story than the war itself.

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:21.610
And this is the one I
want to explore and share.

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:24.500
For The Enemy, I am using
the latest technologies

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:28.280
and virtual augmented realities
so you can engage directly

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:32.060
with the combatants and meet
them, hear them, and feel them

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:34.640
the way I did.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:38.810
In many parts of our world,
you create an enemy as a kid

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:42.320
without having met your enemy,
because the society around you

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:44.150
has created an
enemy in the other.

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:51.210
So the question is, could it be
you if I was on the other side?

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:02.989
[END PLAYBACK]

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:07.410
So I guess to start
the conversation,

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:10.650
I would love to just ask
you what brought you to MIT,

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:13.260
and how did your
experience collaborating

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:16.050
with professor Fox Harrell
and the MIT community

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:18.100
impact your work?

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:22.810
Well, I was invited at MIT
by William Uricchio and Sarah

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:25.360
Wolozin at the Open
Doc Lab, so this

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:27.450
that was my first introduction.

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:30.930
And then from there,
everything took kind of sense,

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:35.040
even though now, looking
back, I could see

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.253
how transformative it's been.

00:04:37.253 --> 00:04:39.420
I think the first thing and
the most important thing

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:43.560
for me has been the ability to
break all the walls that I had

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:46.320
constructed around
my work and how

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:49.200
I was looking at storytelling.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:52.740
And then being in an environment
where everything is challenged,

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:56.060
whatever is the status
quo is challenged,

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:59.220
have me challenge my own
journalism, and then with this,

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:00.900
using new technologies.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.020
But most importantly-- new
technologies are there,

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:05.820
and they keep evolving,
but what was I

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:09.030
guess the richest part of
all was my collaboration

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:11.790
with Professor Fox Harrell.

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:14.490
And in that sense,
as a journalist

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:19.710
and him as a scientist, we
have different sets of ethics.

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:22.920
And it was so interesting
to share those and see

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:25.870
how science works and
how a journalist works

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:28.440
and what we can do, on one
side, and what we can't, and

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:32.190
in finding a bridge in between
in order to do something

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.850
that had not been done so far.

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:38.790
And this had profoundly
changed the work, my ability

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:42.240
to really push the boundaries
further and then learn

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:46.450
a great deal on the process.

00:05:46.450 --> 00:05:48.640
It's been about, I
think, six or seven years

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:52.000
since we first met through
Fox's Advanced Identity

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:53.930
Representation course.

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:56.680
And I remember just being struck
by the lineage of the Enemy

00:05:56.680 --> 00:06:00.340
project, from it being sort
of a photo exhibit, all

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:03.680
the way to becoming a virtual
reality exhibition and an AR

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:04.540
app.

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:07.000
Could you share a
little bit about--

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:09.730
you said something along
the lines of stories

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:11.995
or how you make sense of
the world and experiences

00:06:11.995 --> 00:06:14.620
or how are how we remember them.

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:16.450
Could you share how
these different media

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:20.556
forms have influence, how
you tell the story over time?

00:06:20.556 --> 00:06:22.828
It influences in a lot of ways.

00:06:22.828 --> 00:06:25.120
When you're a photojournalist
and you do a photo story,

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:27.598
you already have a
distribution in place.

00:06:27.598 --> 00:06:29.140
You work for magazines,
the magazines

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:30.400
have their own distribution.

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:32.440
There's a lot you don't
need to think about.

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:34.045
And as a war
correspondent, you really

00:06:34.045 --> 00:06:35.920
try to concentrate on
the story you're doing,

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:37.858
knowing you have the
magazines behind you.

00:06:37.858 --> 00:06:40.150
But as you evolve and as you
start thinking differently

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:42.670
about your own
work, it is always

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:47.890
important to use the best
medium for the best audience.

00:06:47.890 --> 00:06:50.380
And so in that sense,
where I was medium-based

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:53.430
for a long time, I am not
a medium-based anymore,

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:55.690
and I think the big
transformation is there.

00:06:55.690 --> 00:06:58.810
The way I'm thinking
is, what is the issue

00:06:58.810 --> 00:07:03.370
that I want to cover that I
think needs to be put in light?

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:06.120
And then once I've
got an issue, I

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:08.790
wonder who should
listen to that issue

00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:10.290
in order to have an impact.

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:13.320
Who are the best audiences that
should be confronted to this?

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:15.320
And sometimes it might
be the local people,

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:18.150
and sometimes there
might be stakeholders.

00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:19.770
And those are
different strategies,

00:07:19.770 --> 00:07:22.770
in order to get to one
place or the other one.

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:26.430
And so I think in that sense,
thinking, what is my project,

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:28.980
who's my audience,
and then finally,

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:31.050
what is the best medium
to reach that audience?

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:32.520
So if you do--

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:36.630
it completely transformed my way
of thinking about my own work,

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:39.130
in that sense that my
audience comes first.

00:07:39.130 --> 00:07:40.740
I'm audience-centered now.

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:44.610
And then the medium comes only
after finding what is the best

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:47.370
medium for that audience.

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:49.890
That's a great segue to
my next question for you

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:53.740
in talking about the audience
and how they receive your work.

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:55.680
So my next question
is, can you tell us

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:58.890
a little bit about the impact
of The Enemy Virtual Reality

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:01.530
Exhibition and the
augmented reality app?

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:03.210
And more specifically,
maybe you could

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:06.300
share some of the feedback
you've received from those

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:08.250
who have experienced The Enemy.

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:09.180
Yeah.

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:12.300
I mean, you talked about
the experience before.

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:14.400
I wasn't aware I was
doing an experience.

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:16.260
VR was creating the experience.

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:18.030
I wasn't creating
the experience,

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:20.370
I was just filling
up the blocks inside,

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:24.300
and the medium in itself
is way more experiential.

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:27.420
So in that sense, it is
always very important

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:30.750
to focus on what is
that you want to say

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:34.140
and how you want
to put it through.

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:38.580
And having different
experiences in different places

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:41.789
with different audiences
is always really--

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:45.010
so when it comes to the
impact, as a journalist,

00:08:45.010 --> 00:08:46.760
I've never been asked
if I have an impact.

00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:49.410
As a human being, I've
wanted to have one.

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:51.650
And so this is a marker
that I've put out there

00:08:51.650 --> 00:08:55.520
in trying to do it, because
it's becoming experiential,

00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:58.618
because the work has become,
through VR and through walking

00:08:58.618 --> 00:09:00.410
the 300-meter square
and meeting the people

00:09:00.410 --> 00:09:03.250
and having the choice to
work in different places.

00:09:03.250 --> 00:09:05.780
And it's not a VR with a
joystick in that sense.

00:09:05.780 --> 00:09:08.030
It's really you
physically moving.

00:09:08.030 --> 00:09:11.420
I think this is what
creates the experience.

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:14.045
And the experience is what
eventually creates the impact.

00:09:16.670 --> 00:09:20.540
I mean, the feedbacks I had from
my audience were always more,

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:22.220
this is an experience.

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:24.810
They remember it, and they
spoke about it as an experience,

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:26.690
as a meeting they
had with fighters,

00:09:26.690 --> 00:09:29.220
while they didn't really
have those meetings.

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:32.660
But everything that was in
place here within the work

00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:34.310
was simulating that meeting.

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:35.720
And I think it creates--

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:38.360
when you have your own choice,
when you can navigate to left,

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:40.610
right, listen to the people,
they look at you directly

00:09:40.610 --> 00:09:43.730
in the eye, it creates a
different level of experience,

00:09:43.730 --> 00:09:47.320
and then, therefore, a
different kind of impact.

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:49.740
And impact is different
things for different people.

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:54.390
I think when you cover wars
and enemies the way I do,

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:56.480
the ultimate impact would
be to stop that war.

00:09:56.480 --> 00:09:59.180
But that's a bit too ambitious.

00:09:59.180 --> 00:10:01.430
I would call my
work being much more

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:03.440
of a journalistic intervention.

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:06.675
Journalism is something that
needs to bring you information

00:10:06.675 --> 00:10:09.050
that you didn't have, in order
to make a better decision.

00:10:11.830 --> 00:10:14.600
I call it an intervention,
because people go 50 minutes

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:18.500
into this VR experience,
and within the 50 minutes,

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:20.540
they've almost forgotten
they are in VR.

00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:23.840
So once they reach back
life as they knew it before,

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:25.310
they've been charged with this.

00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:27.170
They've been charged
with the experience.

00:10:27.170 --> 00:10:31.220
And I think it does change
a sense of the perspective.

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:32.780
It's a seed that you put.

00:10:32.780 --> 00:10:35.030
But it's hard to tell
if that seed's going

00:10:35.030 --> 00:10:39.920
to bring the largest and highest
oak you've seen in your life,

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:43.310
or if it just stays there
and never really sprouts.

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:45.570
So in that sense, it's
hard to really [INAUDIBLE]..

00:10:45.570 --> 00:10:49.350
But we've done a few interviews
with users and especially

00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:55.280
interactive, where we could
show you now perhaps what

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:58.310
one of the users had
to say about The Enemy

00:10:58.310 --> 00:11:00.873
walking out of this.

00:11:00.873 --> 00:11:01.540
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:11:01.540 --> 00:11:03.700
- [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

00:11:55.057 --> 00:11:55.640
[END PLAYBACK]

00:11:55.640 --> 00:11:58.950
You see, this is really what
I'm talking about, a seed.

00:11:58.950 --> 00:12:02.130
Walking out of this,
he was really surprised

00:12:02.130 --> 00:12:05.820
to have encountered his
enemy and to see similarities

00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:06.990
with his enemy.

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:08.900
In order to kill, you
need to dehumanize.

00:12:08.900 --> 00:12:10.980
And my work was really
about rehumanizing

00:12:10.980 --> 00:12:14.370
and trying to rehumanize
the other and finding not--

00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:16.110
not working on the
differences, but what

00:12:16.110 --> 00:12:19.500
is similar in between them
and their basic humanity.

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:20.513
Yes, you face killers.

00:12:20.513 --> 00:12:21.930
Yes, you face
people who have been

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:24.120
involved in very violent acts.

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:27.360
But at the same time, they
still have always some parts

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:29.183
and somewhere some humanity.

00:12:29.183 --> 00:12:30.600
And if you want
to make peace, you

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:33.000
need to reconcile with
that humanity in your enemy

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:35.830
to start with, and then
fix everything then after.

00:12:35.830 --> 00:12:38.730
So I think that's
the first step.

00:12:38.730 --> 00:12:42.840
We can see, as a matter
of fact, an Israeli who's

00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:47.070
going to be drafted and go a
few months later in the army.

00:12:47.070 --> 00:12:50.130
So this was an intervention,
because he realized something

00:12:50.130 --> 00:12:54.830
about his enemy he wouldn't
have realized otherwise.

00:12:54.830 --> 00:12:57.110
Thank you so much
for sharing that.

00:12:57.110 --> 00:13:00.380
On that same thread of
planting seeds and watching

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:02.600
them grow within
your audiences, I'd

00:13:02.600 --> 00:13:05.450
love to know what
projects are you currently

00:13:05.450 --> 00:13:08.300
or looking forward
to working on next?

00:13:08.300 --> 00:13:11.360
So let's back up just a second.

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:13.700
It wasn't that long ago.

00:13:13.700 --> 00:13:15.700
What is the story
I want to do, who's

00:13:15.700 --> 00:13:18.090
the audience I want
to engage with,

00:13:18.090 --> 00:13:20.750
and what is the best
medium to reach them?

00:13:20.750 --> 00:13:26.860
So I'd gone to Congo
in 2015 for The Enemy.

00:13:26.860 --> 00:13:28.330
I went to see the mines.

00:13:28.330 --> 00:13:32.990
And I went to see a goldmine
where people were scratching

00:13:32.990 --> 00:13:34.420
with their hands to the ground.

00:13:34.420 --> 00:13:38.500
It's really a part of
the hill was torn apart.

00:13:38.500 --> 00:13:42.820
And I photographed there, and
I photographed with my iPhone.

00:13:42.820 --> 00:13:45.550
And suddenly it
struck me that I was

00:13:45.550 --> 00:13:51.460
using the minerals they were
mining, and it was in my phone.

00:13:51.460 --> 00:13:53.140
And I was using them
to photograph them.

00:13:53.140 --> 00:13:56.770
So I started thinking, I need
to do something about that.

00:13:56.770 --> 00:13:57.670
It's an old story.

00:13:57.670 --> 00:14:01.000
We know within our electronics
we have so many rare earth

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:02.770
minerals, and they
come from countries

00:14:02.770 --> 00:14:04.550
that sometimes are problematic.

00:14:04.550 --> 00:14:07.550
And the DRC is
definitely problematic.

00:14:07.550 --> 00:14:10.360
And so I'm trying to create
a link between what's

00:14:10.360 --> 00:14:13.180
happening in Congo and you
having a phone in your hands.

00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:14.950
And I'm using augmented reality.

00:14:14.950 --> 00:14:16.990
You're going to be
able to visit all

00:14:16.990 --> 00:14:19.210
the parts and the
components of your phone

00:14:19.210 --> 00:14:22.990
and see where those minerals are
exactly located in your phone

00:14:22.990 --> 00:14:25.030
and why we're using
them, and definitely

00:14:25.030 --> 00:14:26.350
why they're indispensable.

00:14:26.350 --> 00:14:29.380
And then from there, we're
going to be trickling down back

00:14:29.380 --> 00:14:32.350
to the mine, where
we realize, what

00:14:32.350 --> 00:14:35.200
is the reality on the ground
for the miners that are working?

00:14:35.200 --> 00:14:37.930
They're often dying in
our horrendous conditions,

00:14:37.930 --> 00:14:40.480
human rights abuse,
workers abuse,

00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:44.830
if not armed groups taking
over some parts in some mines

00:14:44.830 --> 00:14:46.870
and then financing
their own fights

00:14:46.870 --> 00:14:49.557
with the benefit of the mine.

00:14:52.180 --> 00:14:55.040
As somebody who worked
in computer science,

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:57.020
I'm struck by how
much ethics have

00:14:57.020 --> 00:15:00.470
come into the conversation in
research circles and industry

00:15:00.470 --> 00:15:01.700
circles.

00:15:01.700 --> 00:15:04.850
But I think taking a step
back and being really critical

00:15:04.850 --> 00:15:07.940
about the tools with which
we tackle these issues is

00:15:07.940 --> 00:15:09.320
incredibly powerful.

00:15:09.320 --> 00:15:11.547
So I look forward
to seeing that work.

00:15:11.547 --> 00:15:12.380
Thank you very much.

00:15:12.380 --> 00:15:16.700
Yeah, it's really trying to
wake a younger generation

00:15:16.700 --> 00:15:21.080
that are using, natively, those
electronics and then reminding

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:24.140
them of those people that
are mining those minerals.

00:15:24.140 --> 00:15:26.180
And it doesn't have
to be that bloody.

00:15:26.180 --> 00:15:29.120
It doesn't have to
carry so much injustice.

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:31.190
I'm looking at my
phone as something

00:15:31.190 --> 00:15:32.403
that enables injustice.

00:15:32.403 --> 00:15:34.070
That's not the way
we look at our phone.

00:15:34.070 --> 00:15:37.100
It's the way we want to
look at our electronics.

00:15:37.100 --> 00:15:40.370
But they do, as of today,
and us as a consumer

00:15:40.370 --> 00:15:42.680
can request to stop this.

00:15:42.680 --> 00:15:45.890
We have the right to not
have blood in our phones.

00:15:45.890 --> 00:15:47.343
And we should, in that sense--

00:15:47.343 --> 00:15:49.010
this is where my work
is going, and this

00:15:49.010 --> 00:15:51.710
is what I'm going to be
proposing to the audience

00:15:51.710 --> 00:15:55.190
within the work is to also have
the sense of vengeance and say,

00:15:55.190 --> 00:15:57.210
I don't like that, and
I want to do something.

00:15:57.210 --> 00:15:59.770
And we propose them
to do something--

00:15:59.770 --> 00:16:02.090
impact again.

00:16:02.090 --> 00:16:04.640
Thank you so much, Karim,
for taking the time

00:16:04.640 --> 00:16:07.130
to talk to our community today.

00:16:07.130 --> 00:16:10.370
I'd love to pass it back to
Catherine to close the series,

00:16:10.370 --> 00:16:13.460
and I appreciate everyone
who joined us on this webinar

00:16:13.460 --> 00:16:14.360
today.

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:16.230
Thank you, Danielle.

00:16:16.230 --> 00:16:17.300
Thank you, Karim.

00:16:17.300 --> 00:16:19.860
This was a great conversation.

00:16:19.860 --> 00:16:22.730
Thanks to everyone who joined
us online, our audience.

00:16:22.730 --> 00:16:25.460
Please join us next
